WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.460
CPU-bound parallelism and I/O-bound asynchrony need different design choices.

2
00:00:05.560 --> 00:00:10.920
CPU-bound work competes for processor time and may benefit from measured parallelism.

3
00:00:11.020 --> 00:00:21.180
I/O-bound work spends much of its time waiting on external systems and usually benefits from asynchronous APIs and concurrency limits rather than raw thread creation.

4
00:00:21.460 --> 00:00:34.740
Parallel loops, fan-out, and batch processing can overwhelm CPUs, database connection pools, APIs, file systems, queues, caches, and downstream services if they are unbounded.

5
00:00:34.840 --> 00:00:38.440
More simultaneous work is not always more throughput.

6
00:00:38.540 --> 00:00:44.220
Past a certain point, the system spends its time waiting, contending, retrying, or timing out.

7
00:00:44.500 --> 00:00:51.780
Background work should have ownership, lifetime, cancellation, error handling, logging, and retry policy.

8
00:00:51.880 --> 00:01:01.640
Work that starts in the background without an owner can outlive the request scope, lose user context, continue after cancellation, or fail without visible evidence.

9
00:01:01.740 --> 00:01:06.780
If work must continue later, a queue or hosted service should own that lifecycle deliberately.

10
00:01:07.060 --> 00:01:14.420
ASP.NET Core request handling should not start unmanaged background work that depends on request-scoped services after the request ends.

11
00:01:14.520 --> 00:01:21.560
DbContext instances, scoped dependencies, user context, and request cancellation tokens have lifetimes.

12
00:01:21.660 --> 00:01:27.100
Sending them into unmanaged background work creates correctness and reliability risks.

13
00:01:27.380 --> 00:01:31.540
Bounded concurrency protects availability and dependency health.

14
00:01:31.640 --> 00:01:41.000
A SemaphoreSlim, bounded channel, worker pool, rate limit, or queue setting can keep the number of active operations within a range the system can support.

15
00:01:41.100 --> 00:01:46.620
The correct bound is an engineering decision based on dependency capacity and production measurement.

16
00:01:46.900 --> 00:01:55.220
Shared service clients, DbContext instances, caches, random generators, and other dependencies need thread-safety review.

17
00:01:55.320 --> 00:01:58.120
Some types are intended to be reused concurrently.

18
00:01:58.220 --> 00:02:01.180
Some are intended to be scoped to a unit of work.

19
00:02:01.280 --> 00:02:03.760
Some require their own synchronization.

20
00:02:03.860 --> 00:02:07.380
The dependency contract should drive the concurrency design.

21
00:02:07.660 --> 00:02:11.660
Resource controls should be measured and tuned in production.

22
00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:24.080
Queue length, latency, error rate, thread pool behavior, dependency saturation, retry volume, and timeout trends tell the team whether the design is operating inside safe limits.
